Sure! Here's a sample node.js script that reads a .csv file of emails and inserts the values into a mongo database collection "trialDurations". The script uses dot-env to find the mongo address and Mongoose to connect and insert data into the database.

First, make sure you have installed the necessary dependencies:


```
npm install mongoose dotenv csv-parser
```
Then, create a .env file in the root directory of your project with the following content:


```makefile
MONGO_URI=your_mongo_uri_here
```
Replace "your\_mongo\_uri\_here" with the URI of your MongoDB database. For example, "mongodb://localhost:27017/mydatabase".

Here's the script:


```javascript
const fs = require('fs');
const csv = require('csv-parser');
const mongoose = require('mongoose');
require('dotenv').config();

// Define the schema for the "trialDurations" collection
const trialDurationSchema = new mongoose.Schema({
 email: {
 type: String,
 required: true,
 },
});

// Create a model for the "trialDurations" collection
const TrialDuration = mongoose.model('trialDurations', trialDurationSchema);

// Connect to the MongoDB database using the Mongoose library
mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO\_URI, {
 useNewUrlParser: true,
 useUnifiedTopology: true,
});

// Read the CSV file of emails and insert each email into the "trialDurations" collection
fs.createReadStream('emails.csv')
 .pipe(csv())
 .on('data', async (data) => {
 // Create a new document using the email value from the CSV row
 const document = new TrialDuration({ email: data.email });
