One way to inverse the order of two buttons when they stack on a responsive webpage is by using CSS Flexbox. Here's an example:

HTML:


```css
<div class="button-container">
 <button class="button1">Button 1</button>
 <button class="button2">Button 2</button>
</div>
```
CSS:


```css
.button-container {
 display: flex;
 flex-direction: column;
}

@media (min-width: 768px) {
 .button-container {
 flex-direction: row;
 justify-content: flex-end;
 }

 .button2 {
 order: -1;
 }
}
```
In this example, the buttons are wrapped in a container div with a flex display and column direction. This means that the buttons will stack vertically on smaller screens.

When the screen width reaches 768 pixels or more, a media query is triggered, and the flex direction of the container is changed to row, which means that the buttons will be positioned side by side. The justify-content property is set to flex-end, which aligns the buttons to the right of the container.

To inverse the order of the buttons, we set the order property of the button2 to -1, which places it before button1 in the flex order. This means that button2 will be displayed to the left of button1 when the buttons are positioned side by side.

