Here's an example of how you could create an infinite scrolling lazy `VStack` in SwiftUI:


```scss
struct ContentView: View {
 @State private var items = Array(0..<100)
 var body: some View {
 ScrollView {
 VStack(spacing: 10) {
 ForEach(items, id: \.self) { item in
 Text("Item \(item)")
 .frame(maxWidth: .infinity, maxHeight: 50)
 .background(Color.orange)
 .foregroundColor(.white)
 }
 }
 .onAppear {
 DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 2) {
 self.items.append(contentsOf: Array(100..<200))
 }
 }
 }
 }
}
```
This implementation uses a `ForEach` view to loop over the items in the `items` array and display a `Text` view for each one. The `VStack` is placed inside a `ScrollView` so that you can scroll through the items.

When the `ContentView` appears on the screen, the `onAppear` closure is executed. In this example, after a delay of 2 seconds, it appends more items to the `items` array, causing the `VStack` to update and display more items.

This creates the illusion of an infinite scrolling `VStack`, although it's not actually infinite. To make it truly infinite, you would need to load new data dynamically as the user scrolls down, but this example demonstrates the basic idea.

